# Styling: Tailwind CSS 4 + daisyUI 5 + Inspira UI

Docs: https://tailwindcss.com/docs/theme , https://daisyui.com/docs/config/ , https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/styling/ , https://inspira-ui.com/docs/en/getting-started/installation

## One stylesheet, CSS-first configuration

- `src/styles/global.css` is the only global stylesheet and is imported once in `BaseLayout.astro`. It holds `@import 'tailwindcss'`, `@import 'tw-animate-css'`, the daisyUI `@plugin` block, the Inspira backgrounds plugin, `@theme` tokens, and `@layer base` resets.
- There is no `tailwind.config.js`. Configure Tailwind in CSS: `@theme` for tokens that should become utilities, `@utility` for custom utilities, `@layer components` only for patterns repeated in many places that daisyUI does not cover.
- Add a design token to `@theme` once; never hard-code brand hex values in markup.

## daisyUI usage

- Use daisyUI components for foundations: `btn`, `card`, `badge`, `navbar`, `footer`, `drawer`, `collapse`, `modal`, `input`, `textarea`, `select`, `checkbox`, `alert`, `breadcrumbs`, `steps`, `tabs`. For rendered Markdown, write article styles in an Astro component or add `@tailwindcss/typography` (`prose`) only when long-form article styling is built; it is not installed yet, so record the decision in `docs/architecture.md`.
- Use daisyUI semantic colors (`bg-base-100`, `text-base-content`, `bg-primary`, `text-primary-content`, `border-base-300`) rather than raw palette colors, so every color comes from the theme.
- Light only (owner's decision, 2026-11-17): the site renders in daisyUI's `light` theme whatever the visitor's device or browser prefers. Do not add a dark theme, `--prefersdark`, `prefers-color-scheme` queries, `dark:` classes, or a theme toggle without a new decision; the smoke suite fails if the page changes under a dark preference.
- How light-only holds, all four together: daisyUI loads only `light --default`; `BaseLayout.astro` sets `data-theme="light"` on `<html>` and `<meta name="color-scheme" content="only light">`; `global.css` sets `color-scheme: only light` on `:root`, unlayered so it beats the plain `light` daisyUI's theme sets in the base layer (with plain `light`, Chromium's forced dark mode, Chrome's "Darken websites", still recolours the page); and Tailwind's `dark:` variant is bound to `[data-theme=dark]`, which nothing sets.
- Primary calls to action render through `src/components/ui/Button.astro`: a pill-shaped `btn btn-primary` with the lit finish from the `btn-lit` utility in `global.css` (gradient, four shadows, flipped on press). Apply `btn-lit` by hand only where an Astro component cannot be used, such as inside a Vue island. `variant="secondary"` gives the next-best action beside a primary one: `btn-outline btn-secondary`, transparent with the owner's near-black label and 1px border, which fills near-black with a white label under the pointer, with no shadow in any state. On light backgrounds give these buttons a near-black focus ring (`focus-visible:outline-neutral`): daisyUI's ring takes the button colour, and the yellow is 1.4:1 there.
- daisyUI's `aura` (border light) always carries one of the two motion utilities in `global.css`; a bare `aura` circles forever and breaks the five-second motion limit. `aura-lap` (`Button.astro`'s `aura`) makes one lap on appearance, then lights only on hover or keyboard focus; let at most one lap play per view. `aura-hover` (`aura="hover"`, the home hero's primary) runs nothing until hover or keyboard focus and goes out at once after, and its wrapper takes no room, so the button renders exactly as it does without the light. The light takes the surrounding text colour, so set a `text-*` colour on the parent when the surface is light. A light that fades while it stands still (`aura-lap`'s after a hover) fades through `--aura-light`, its strength in the gradient, never through `color`: Safari without WebKit's fix of 2026-10-09 does not repaint a gradient whose only change is currentColor, so the light stayed on. The aura raises the button it wraps above static siblings: make neighbours its glow reaches `relative` (the hero row's `*:relative`) so they still paint above it.
- Brand palette: only the owner decides brand colours. Decided so far: primary `#f7db15` (2026-11-17), the colour of every primary call to action, with the `neutral` near-black as its label (white on it is 1.4:1); secondary `#141817` (2026-11-17), the label and border of the secondary call to action and its fill under the pointer, where the label turns white. Each decided colour goes in the `@plugin 'daisyui/theme' { name: 'light'; default: true; ... }` block in `global.css`: daisyUI merges a block named after a built-in theme into that theme's tokens, so `data-theme="light"`, the smoke test, and the light-only setup stay as they are. Record each colour in the "Brand colours" row of `docs/architecture.md`; every colour not yet decided stays at daisyUI's `light` value.

## Markup rules

- Mobile-first: write base styles for small screens, then `md:` and `lg:` overrides.
- No inline `style=""` except for truly dynamic values; no `!important` in markup.
- Keep class lists readable: layout, spacing, typography, color, state. Extract a component when the same combination appears three times.
- Spacing and sizing come from the Tailwind scale; arbitrary values (`w-[437px]`) need a comment explaining why.
- Focus styles must stay visible: never add `outline-none` without a replacement `focus-visible:` style.

## Inspira UI

- Inspira components are copied into `src/components/inspira/` from the official docs, keeping their file names, then adapted. They import `cn` from `@/lib/utils`. Installed support packages: `@vueuse/core`, `motion-v`, `tw-animate-css`, `@inspira-ui/plugins` (background utilities loaded via `src/styles/inspira-backgrounds.mjs`).
- Some components depend on additional pieces (shadcn-vue primitives, `reka-ui`, icons). Read the component's import list before copying; add a dependency only when the component is approved, and note it in `docs/architecture.md`.
- Use effects purposefully: at most one or two per page, on hero backgrounds, section dividers, or interactive cards. Never animate body text, navigation, or form controls. Every effect must respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (global CSS gives every animation and transition zero duration and delay, so nothing moves; also skip JS-driven loops when reduced motion is on).
- Effects must not shift layout or block interaction. Hydrate with `client:visible` or `client:idle`, and keep each effect's JavaScript small enough that Lighthouse performance stays above 90 on mobile.

## Fonts and icons

- Default to system fonts via the `--font-sans` token. If a brand web font is adopted, self-host it in `src/assets/fonts/` or use Astro's font support, with `font-display: swap`, and document the choice.
- No web font is loaded, so text renders in each visitor's system font: SF Pro on Apple devices, Segoe UI on Windows, Roboto on Android, and on Linux usually DejaVu Sans, the widest (it is also what CI renders). Size anything that must stay on one line for DejaVu Sans; the hero headline's comment in `src/components/sections/Hero.astro` lists measured widths.
- Icons: Lucide only (proposed 2026-11-18 for the home service cards, awaiting the owner's confirmation). Its SVG files are copied unchanged into `src/assets/icons/` (no icon package is installed), listed in `src/lib/icons.ts`, and drawn with `src/components/ui/Icon.astro`, which inlines them at build time: no JavaScript, icon font, or request. They are decorative (`aria-hidden`), so the text beside an icon carries its meaning; an icon-only control needs an `aria-label`. Add an icon from the same Lucide release and extend its entry in `src/assets/CREDITS.md`; never mix in another family or emoji.

## Responsive QA

- Check every layout change at 360, 390, 768, 1024, 1440, and 1920 px with `npx playwright test tests/e2e/layout.spec.ts --project=desktop-chromium`, then inspect the screenshots it writes to `reports/screenshots/`. Procedure: `.cursor/skills/responsive-qa/SKILL.md`.
- Prevent overflow at the source: `min-w-0` on flex children, `break-words` for long strings, `max-w-*` containers, fluid images (`w-full h-auto` with explicit dimensions), responsive grids. Never hide overflow to mask a layout bug.
- Mobile projects in Playwright are emulations; physical-device checks are a separate, manual step before launch.

## Checks for this area

`npx prettier --check <files>`, `npm run build` (Tailwind errors surface at build), `npm run test:e2e` (axe catches contrast and focus regressions).
